// T4 gate: does a map free the KEYS it owns?
//
// PR #269 gave the release path a destructor, so a released map frees its
// entry CHAIN. It does not free keys, because goo_map_set_sv stores the
// caller's pointer verbatim (`e->key = k;`) and the map therefore owns no key
// storage. `goo_map_delete_sv` documents that contract.
//
// This probe covers the case where the map CAN own the key: the key expression
// is a fresh temporary that no other name ever held. strings.ToUpper is
// non_retaining = 1 in src/types/shim_signatures.c, verified against
// goo_strings_map_case, which goo_allocs and writes per byte. So each key here
// is a fresh allocation with exactly one reference, and the map is the only
// thing that can free it.
//
// `m` itself is release-eligible: bound once at function scope to a map
// literal (owned), no arena, and nothing derived from it leaves build --
// len(m) yields an int. So the release site exists and the destructor runs.
//
// WHAT THE GATE MUST READ. With the release OFF the map leaks, so its entries
// leak with it and the keys are INDIRECTLY lost. With the release ON but no key
// ownership, the map and its entries are freed and the keys become DIRECTLY
// lost -- the same bytes, a different valgrind category. A gate reading only
// `definitely lost` would therefore see this probe's bytes GO UP when the map
// destructor started working, and call a correct change a regression. Read
// definite AND indirect, from the SUMMARY lines. This exact partial read has
// produced a wrong answer three times on this arc.
package main

import "strings"

var sink int

// Two keys per call, both fresh. 10,000 calls, so the difference is measurable
// rather than theoretical.
func build(n int) int {
	m := map[string]int{}
	m[strings.ToUpper("alpha")] = n
	m[strings.ToUpper("beta")] = n
	return len(m)
}

func main() {
	total := 0
	for i := 0; i < 10000; i++ {
		total = total + build(i)
	}
	sink = total
	// Printed so the gate can diff this against the GOO_ARC_RELEASE=0 control.
	// PR #264 measured a substring release that valgrind reported as clean
	// while it silently corrupted eight bytes of a live string, so a
	// valgrind-only check has no teeth against a whole class of defect.
	println(total)
}
